<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomon II of Imereti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Solomon II of Imereti was the last king of Imereti in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, reigning from 1789 to 1810. He accepted a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1804 but resisted direct rule. In 1810 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> deposed him and annexed Imereti; he escaped to Ottoman territory and died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon-ii-of-imereti.jpg" alt="Solomon II of Imereti" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: archives.gov.ge · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solomon_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Solomon was born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>, the son of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archil-of-imereti">Archil of Imereti</a>, brother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I of Imereti</a>, and of Helen, a daughter of King Heraclius II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Solomon I died in 1784 without a male heir and named his nephew David as his successor. Another uncle, David II, kept both him and a rival prince, George, from the crown and seized the throne himself, which led to a civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Heraclius II sent an army on behalf of his grandson and defeated David II at the Battle of Matkhoji on 11 June 1789, after which David was crowned as Solomon II. David II kept up his attempts to regain the throne until his final defeat in 1792. Ruling under his grandfather's protection, Solomon continued Solomon I's policy of curbing the powers of the feudal aristocracy. In 1795 he and Heraclius fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> with a small Imeretian force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-krtsanisi">Battle of Krtsanisi</a> and were completely defeated.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> in 1798 and the Russian annexation of Kartli-Kakheti in 1800, his position grew precarious. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, the princes of Mingrelia and Guria, placed themselves under Russian protection and claimed parts of the royal domains. Solomon sought Ottoman and Persian support, but the Russian commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a>, marched into Imereti and forced him to accept vassalage in the convention of Elaznauri of 25 April 1804.</p><p>Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> stayed strained. On 20 February 1810 the Russian administration removed him from the throne. Solomon rallied the people and appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and Napoleonic France. Insurgents once defeated one of the two Russian units sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, but Lieutenant-General Rozen, helped by Georgian nobles who changed sides, defeated Solomon in the Khanistsqali valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Solomon died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and was buried in the Saint Gregory of Nyssa Church; in 1990 his body was moved to Gelati Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. His marriage to Princess Mariam Dadiani, daughter of Katsia Dadiani, Prince of Mingrelia, was childless. Guided by his spiritual teacher Hilarion, he strengthened the Church, granted lands and tax privileges to the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in Imereti and reissued the charter of the Bichvinta Cathedral. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a> canonised him as a saint on 27 July 2005.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solomon II of Imereti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Solomon I died in 1784, his successor David II struggled with rival claimants, and in 1789 <b>Solomon II</b>, a grandson of Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti, took the throne with eastern Georgian support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.27,42.71&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Kutaisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5586 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Solomon II of Imereti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6036 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.27,42.71&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
